{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Norman ruler who ruled over the County of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Pons of Tripoli and was born around 1135. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Crusades and played a significant role in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was born in 1135 and was the son of Pons of Tripoli who ruled over the County of Tripoli from 1121 until his death in 1152. After the death of his father Raymond III succeeded him as the ruler of the county. He was a skilled military leader and expanded the county's borders through various military campaigns. In 1152 Raymond III married Eschiva of Ibelin and had several children with her including Eschiva of Tripoli and Raymond of Ibelin.\n\n**The Crusades and Military Campaigns**\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Crusades and was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He participated in several military campaigns including the Second Crusade and the Third Crusade. Raymond III was also known for his military prowess and was said to have been killed in battle in 1187 while fighting against Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\n\nDuring his reign Raymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He had to navigate the politics of the Crusader states and was often at odds with other rulers. In 1158 Raymond III was forced to sign a treaty with the Kingdom of Jerusalem which ceded certain territories to the kingdom. However this was not the end of the conflict between Raymond III and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1183 Raymond III clashed with the Kingdom of Jerusalem again and was forced to flee to the County of Tripoli.\n\n**Death and Legacy**\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his son Raymond of Ibelin. He is